# Versions, upgrades and the library pin

`docs/RUNBOOKS.md` § Upgrades is canonical. This page is the two flows an agent
runs most.

## Bumping a version pin

Every chart, image and binary version is single-sourced in
`ansible/inventories/prod/group_vars/all.yml`. Manifests spell them as
`${<name>_version}` and Flux substitutes at reconcile time.

1. `task maintenance:check-versions` lists what is behind.
2. Edit the pin in `all.yml`. Never hand-edit the generated ConfigMap.
3. `task flux:sync-versions`, then commit both files in one commit. The drift
   gate inside `task lint` fails if they disagree.
4. `task flux:lint` if the chart's values changed.
5. Read the release notes for a CRD change. A chart that manages its own CRDs can
   DELETE them on an upgrade or a downgrade, taking every object of that kind
   with it.

Fold every available bump into a revision you are already making rather than
opening a revision just for bumps, and read the pipeline's version-check output
rather than a local run, whose cache can be stale.

A Docker Hub library image (`nextcloud`, `postgres`, `redis`, `valkey`) is
rebuilt in place under the same tag, so pin it by digest beside the version and
couple the two in `version-registry.py`; a release-tagged app image is pinned by
tag alone. Traefik's Cloudflare trusted-proxy list is the same shape:
`check-cloudflare-ips.py` reds the release when the published ranges move.

## Bumping the library pin

The roles, CI job templates, Terraform modules and vendored gates all come from
weisssrv-lib at `{{ lib_ref }}`. A behaviour change is a library merge request,
then a pin bump here. The whole bump lands in one merge request.

1. Merge the library MR. Its release job cuts the tag from the
   conventional-commit subjects; nobody tags by hand. The library's
   `docs/VERSIONING.md` § Releases are cut automatically owns that.
2. `scripts/check-lib-pins.py --bump vX.Y.Z`. It moves the declared `lib_ref`
   input in `.gitlab-ci.yml` and every pin derived from it: the `include:`
   refs, `ansible/requirements.yml` and each Terraform `?ref=` the
   `scripts/lib-ref-sites.yml` list names.
3. `task lib:sync` refreshes `.weisssrv-lib/` at the new ref. Re-vendor every
   file the manifest lists, which covers more than `scripts/`:

   ```bash
   python3 .weisssrv-lib/scripts/check-vendored-copies.py \
     --repo-root . --lib-path .weisssrv-lib --list
   python3 .weisssrv-lib/scripts/check-vendored-copies.py \
     --repo-root . --lib-path .weisssrv-lib --ref vX.Y.Z --require-ref
   ```

   The first lists what to copy; the second reports what still drifts. A
   declared fork absorbs the library's change, then its `reconciled_sha256` in
   the manifest is re-taken. A vendored copy edited here is reverted by the
   next re-vendor.
4. `scripts/check-role-default-flips.py --from <installed tag> --to vX.Y.Z --lib .weisssrv-lib`
   reports role defaults that flip between the two tags with no inventory value
   pinning them, and fails on one declared for some hosts only. Declare each one
   in the inventory, or pass `--allow <key>` once the new default is adopted on
   purpose. `--allow-partial` accepts the partly declared ones after a review
   that the other hosts want the new default. No pipeline runs it, because it
   needs both tags side by side.
5. Land the inventory changes a renamed or emptied variable needs in the SAME
   merge request. Role variables are `| default(...)`-guarded, so a missed rename
   does not fail: it takes the role default.
6. `task ansible:install-collections` reinstalls the collection at the new pin.
   It passes `--force` itself, because the installer skips a collection that is
   already installed.
7. Run `task lint`, then prove pipeline parity before merging. The library
   serves several consumers, so a changed input default changes this pipeline
   too.

## OS patching and reboots

The maintenance playbooks drain, patch, reboot and uncordon one node at a time,
and the manual CI jobs run them. Do not run two against the same target at once,
and never start a manual deploy while that target's CI deploy job is in flight.
